{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-798",
              "name": "Hardcoded default JWT secret enables authentication bypass and role forgery in local auth mode",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Hardcoded default JWT secret enables authentication bypass and role forgery in local auth mode"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When `JWT_SECRET` is not set in the environment, the backend falls back to a hardcoded, publicly-known default secret (`super-agent-local-dev-secret-change-in-production`) with no startup guard. In local auth mode, any attacker who can reach the API can forge a valid JWT authenticating as any user with any role (including `owner`) and any organization ID."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When `JWT_SECRET` is not set in the environment, the backend falls back to a hardcoded, publicly-known default secret (`super-agent-local-dev-secret-change-in-production`) with no startup guard. In local auth mode, any attacker who can reach the API can forge a valid JWT authenticating as any user with any role (including `owner`) and any organization ID.\n\nComplete authentication bypass on any deployment that does not set `JWT_SECRET`: the attacker authenticates as an arbitrary existing user (profile lookup by `sub`) with full `owner` role and an attacker-chosen `orgId`, granting read/write access to all organizations' data, agents, workflows, API keys, and admin functions.\n\nRemove the default value for `JWT_SECRET` in the zod schema and fail startup when it is unset (or when it equals the known default) in production; require an operator-provided secret of sufficient entropy.",
                "markdown": "When `JWT_SECRET` is not set in the environment, the backend falls back to a hardcoded, publicly-known default secret (`super-agent-local-dev-secret-change-in-production`) with no startup guard. In local auth mode, any attacker who can reach the API can forge a valid JWT authenticating as any user with any role (including `owner`) and any organization ID.\n\nComplete authentication bypass on any deployment that does not set `JWT_SECRET`: the attacker authenticates as an arbitrary existing user (profile lookup by `sub`) with full `owner` role and an attacker-chosen `orgId`, granting read/write access to all organizations' data, agents, workflows, API keys, and admin functions.\n\nRemove the default value for `JWT_SECRET` in the zod schema and fail startup when it is unset (or when it equals the known default) in production; require an operator-provided secret of sufficient entropy."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "8.1",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-798",
                  "stride:S",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/798.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-639",
              "name": "Cross-scope membership manipulation via unvalidated membershipId in scope-membership routes",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Cross-scope membership manipulation via unvalidated membershipId in scope-membership routes"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The scope-membership management routes authorize on the scope in the URL path but mutate a membership record chosen by an unvalidated :membershipId parameter, allowing an authenticated user who is an admin of any one scope to change or remove members of any other business scope (including cross-organization scopes)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The scope-membership management routes authorize on the scope in the URL path but mutate a membership record chosen by an unvalidated :membershipId parameter, allowing an authenticated user who is an admin of any one scope to change or remove members of any other business scope (including cross-organization scopes).\n\nUnauthorized cross-scope membership manipulation: an attacker can escalate or remove another user's role in a restricted business scope they do not administer, altering who can access that scope's agents, documents and data.\n\nIn the membership update/remove handlers, resolve the membership record first and verify its business_scope_id equals the authorized path :id (and its organization_id equals the caller's org) before updating or deleting; alternatively pass scopeId into scopeAccessService.updateMemberRole/removeMember and enforce it there.",
                "markdown": "The scope-membership management routes authorize on the scope in the URL path but mutate a membership record chosen by an unvalidated :membershipId parameter, allowing an authenticated user who is an admin of any one scope to change or remove members of any other business scope (including cross-organization scopes).\n\nUnauthorized cross-scope membership manipulation: an attacker can escalate or remove another user's role in a restricted business scope they do not administer, altering who can access that scope's agents, documents and data.\n\nIn the membership update/remove handlers, resolve the membership record first and verify its business_scope_id equals the authorized path :id (and its organization_id equals the caller's org) before updating or deleting; alternatively pass scopeId into scopeAccessService.updateMemberRole/removeMember and enforce it there."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "4.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-639",
                  "stride:E"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/639.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-346",
              "name": "Internal service token HMAC uses public Cognito userPoolId as secret, enabling unauthenticated token forgery in cognito mode",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Internal service token HMAC uses public Cognito userPoolId as secret, enabling unauthenticated token forgery in cognito mode"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "When `AUTH_MODE=cognito`, internal service tokens are HMAC-signed with the Cognito `userPoolId` — a public identifier that the backend itself returns on the unauthenticated `GET /api/auth/config` endpoint. Any attacker who can read that endpoint can forge internal tokens authenticating as any user with any role, bypassing profile and membership lookups entirely."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "When `AUTH_MODE=cognito`, internal service tokens are HMAC-signed with the Cognito `userPoolId` — a public identifier that the backend itself returns on the unauthenticated `GET /api/auth/config` endpoint. Any attacker who can read that endpoint can forge internal tokens authenticating as any user with any role, bypassing profile and membership lookups entirely.\n\nIn cognito mode, full authentication bypass as an arbitrary identity/role/org with no database lookups, on every route protected by `authenticate`. The same primitive is what agent subprocesses legitimately use, so the bypass is indistinguishable from a trusted internal caller.\n\nUse a dedicated, operator-provided high-entropy secret (independent of JWT_SECRET and of any Cognito identifier) as the internal token HMAC key, loaded from the environment with no default. Long term, prefer an asymmetric scheme so the API only holds a public key.",
                "markdown": "When `AUTH_MODE=cognito`, internal service tokens are HMAC-signed with the Cognito `userPoolId` — a public identifier that the backend itself returns on the unauthenticated `GET /api/auth/config` endpoint. Any attacker who can read that endpoint can forge internal tokens authenticating as any user with any role, bypassing profile and membership lookups entirely.\n\nIn cognito mode, full authentication bypass as an arbitrary identity/role/org with no database lookups, on every route protected by `authenticate`. The same primitive is what agent subprocesses legitimately use, so the bypass is indistinguishable from a trusted internal caller.\n\nUse a dedicated, operator-provided high-entropy secret (independent of JWT_SECRET and of any Cognito identifier) as the internal token HMAC key, loaded from the environment with no default. Long term, prefer an asymmetric scheme so the API only holds a public key."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-346",
                  "stride:S"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/346.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-598",
              "name": "Session JWT accepted and propagated via ?token= query parameter",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Session JWT accepted and propagated via ?token= query parameter"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The authentication middleware accepts a full session JWT via the `?token=` query parameter on every authenticated route, and the frontend actively embeds it in URLs for iframes, file previews and downloads. Query strings are routinely captured in browser history, server/proxy access logs, and Referer headers, exposing a 24-hour-lived session token."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The authentication middleware accepts a full session JWT via the `?token=` query parameter on every authenticated route, and the frontend actively embeds it in URLs for iframes, file previews and downloads. Query strings are routinely captured in browser history, server/proxy access logs, and Referer headers, exposing a 24-hour-lived session token.\n\nSession token disclosure through URL artifacts (history, logs, Referer) allows an observer with access to those artifacts to reuse the token for up to 24 hours and impersonate the user.\n\nRestrict query-param authentication to the specific embedded-file routes that require it (e.g. via a dedicated middleware on those routes only), issue short-lived, single-purpose tokens for those cases, and stop embedding session JWTs in URLs in the frontend.",
                "markdown": "The authentication middleware accepts a full session JWT via the `?token=` query parameter on every authenticated route, and the frontend actively embeds it in URLs for iframes, file previews and downloads. Query strings are routinely captured in browser history, server/proxy access logs, and Referer headers, exposing a 24-hour-lived session token.\n\nSession token disclosure through URL artifacts (history, logs, Referer) allows an observer with access to those artifacts to reuse the token for up to 24 hours and impersonate the user.\n\nRestrict query-param authentication to the specific embedded-file routes that require it (e.g. via a dedicated middleware on those routes only), issue short-lived, single-purpose tokens for those cases, and stop embedding session JWTs in URLs in the frontend."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-598",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/598.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0007",
              "name": "Unauthenticated IM webhook allows arbitrary agent execution and org session creation (POST /api/im/webhook/:bindingId)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated IM webhook allows arbitrary agent execution and org session creation (POST /api/im/webhook/:bindingId)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The generic IM webhook `POST /api/im/webhook/:bindingId` requires no authentication, no signature, and no shared secret: any network caller who knows (or guesses) a binding ID can submit arbitrary text that is processed by the org's AI agent, can create chat sessions in the victim org, and receives the agent's full response back in the HTTP reply."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The generic IM webhook `POST /api/im/webhook/:bindingId` requires no authentication, no signature, and no shared secret: any network caller who knows (or guesses) a binding ID can submit arbitrary text that is processed by the org's AI agent, can create chat sessions in the victim org, and receives the agent's full response back in the HTTP reply.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker with knowledge of a binding ID can: (1) run the victim organization's AI agent with arbitrary instructions (prompt injection is directly delivered, and the agent's response — which may include workspace/file/credential data the agent can access — is returned in the HTTP response); (2) create new chat sessions in the victim org/scope as the binding creator; (3) send unsolicited messages into the bound IM channel via adapter.sendReply, including in existing sticky sessions (conversing in the org's Slack/Teams/etc. threads); (4) enumerate valid binding IDs via the 404-vs-200 distinction. Impact depends on the binding's agent tooling, which per the platform design can include workspace files and outbound integrations.\n\nRequire an authentication secret on this endpoint before resolving the binding: issue a per-binding signing secret at binding creation and verify an HMAC signature over the raw body (mirroring the existing WhatsApp `x-hub-signature-256` verification) or require an `Authorization` header checked against a per-binding token stored in `binding.config`. Perform the check before `findBindingById` and return an indistinguishable 404/401 for both unknown and invalid-secret cases to remove the enumeration oracle. Do not accept any secret passed as a query parameter.",
                "markdown": "The generic IM webhook `POST /api/im/webhook/:bindingId` requires no authentication, no signature, and no shared secret: any network caller who knows (or guesses) a binding ID can submit arbitrary text that is processed by the org's AI agent, can create chat sessions in the victim org, and receives the agent's full response back in the HTTP reply.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker with knowledge of a binding ID can: (1) run the victim organization's AI agent with arbitrary instructions (prompt injection is directly delivered, and the agent's response — which may include workspace/file/credential data the agent can access — is returned in the HTTP response); (2) create new chat sessions in the victim org/scope as the binding creator; (3) send unsolicited messages into the bound IM channel via adapter.sendReply, including in existing sticky sessions (conversing in the org's Slack/Teams/etc. threads); (4) enumerate valid binding IDs via the 404-vs-200 distinction. Impact depends on the binding's agent tooling, which per the platform design can include workspace files and outbound integrations.\n\nRequire an authentication secret on this endpoint before resolving the binding: issue a per-binding signing secret at binding creation and verify an HMAC signature over the raw body (mirroring the existing WhatsApp `x-hub-signature-256` verification) or require an `Authorization` header checked against a per-binding token stored in `binding.config`. Perform the check before `findBindingById` and return an indistinguishable 404/401 for both unknown and invalid-secret cases to remove the enumeration oracle. Do not accept any secret passed as a query parameter."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "6.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-53550",
              "name": "CVE-2026-53550 in js-yaml 4.1.1 (user-supplied YAML parsing)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-53550 in js-yaml 4.1.1 (user-supplied YAML parsing)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`js-yaml` 4.1.1 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and is covered by advisory CVE-2026-53550 (fixed in 4.2.0). The backend parses user-supplied YAML OpenAPI specs with it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`js-yaml` 4.1.1 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and is covered by advisory CVE-2026-53550 (fixed in 4.2.0). The backend parses user-supplied YAML OpenAPI specs with it.\n\nThe vulnerable js-yaml parser executes on attacker-controlled YAML supplied by any authenticated user via the integration-upload route, so a flaw in the parser is directly triggerable with a low-privilege account.\n\nBump `js-yaml` to at least 4.2.0 in super-agent/backend (direct dependency in package.json, `^4.1.1`) and regenerate package-lock.json.",
                "markdown": "`js-yaml` 4.1.1 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and is covered by advisory CVE-2026-53550 (fixed in 4.2.0). The backend parses user-supplied YAML OpenAPI specs with it.\n\nThe vulnerable js-yaml parser executes on attacker-controlled YAML supplied by any authenticated user via the integration-upload route, so a flaw in the parser is directly triggerable with a low-privilege account.\n\nBump `js-yaml` to at least 4.2.0 in super-agent/backend (direct dependency in package.json, `^4.1.1`) and regenerate package-lock.json."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.4",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CVE-2026-53550",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0010",
              "name": "Unauthenticated WebSocket execution gateway: any client can subscribe to any org's workflow execution events (/ws/executions)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated WebSocket execution gateway: any client can subscribe to any org's workflow execution events (/ws/executions)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The workflow-execution WebSocket endpoint `GET /ws/executions` accepts connections and subscriptions with no authentication and no organization check: any unauthenticated client can subscribe to any execution ID and receive all of that execution's workflow events — including node status, logs, and output data belonging to other users and organizations."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The workflow-execution WebSocket endpoint `GET /ws/executions` accepts connections and subscriptions with no authentication and no organization check: any unauthenticated client can subscribe to any execution ID and receive all of that execution's workflow events — including node status, logs, and output data belonging to other users and organizations.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker who learns or guesses an execution ID can passively observe another organization's workflow execution in real time: node status, execution logs, and event payloads that may contain node output data, intermediate results, or prompt/response content. This is a cross-tenant information disclosure requiring zero credentials.\n\nApply the existing `authenticate` middleware as a `preValidation` hook on the WebSocket route (Fastify preHandler hooks run on WebSocket GET upgrades), and inside `handleSubscribe` verify that the authenticated user's organization matches the organization that owns the execution record (look up the execution by ID and compare org ownership) before adding the subscription. Reject unknown execution IDs with an error indistinguishable from the not-authorized case to avoid an enumeration oracle.",
                "markdown": "The workflow-execution WebSocket endpoint `GET /ws/executions` accepts connections and subscriptions with no authentication and no organization check: any unauthenticated client can subscribe to any execution ID and receive all of that execution's workflow events — including node status, logs, and output data belonging to other users and organizations.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker who learns or guesses an execution ID can passively observe another organization's workflow execution in real time: node status, execution logs, and event payloads that may contain node output data, intermediate results, or prompt/response content. This is a cross-tenant information disclosure requiring zero credentials.\n\nApply the existing `authenticate` middleware as a `preValidation` hook on the WebSocket route (Fastify preHandler hooks run on WebSocket GET upgrades), and inside `handleSubscribe` verify that the authenticated user's organization matches the organization that owns the execution record (look up the execution by ID and compare org ownership) before adding the subscription. Reject unknown execution IDs with an error indistinguishable from the not-authorized case to avoid an enumeration oracle."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-48779",
              "name": "CVE-2026-48779 in ws 8.19.0 (type-only import; no runtime use in backend)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-48779 in ws 8.19.0 (type-only import; no runtime use in backend)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`ws` 8.19.0 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and covered by advisory CVE-2026-48779 (fixed in 8.21.0). Report filed against the sandbox-default backend manifest as instructed."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`ws` 8.19.0 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and covered by advisory CVE-2026-48779 (fixed in 8.21.0). Report filed against the sandbox-default backend manifest as instructed.\n\nNo runtime use of the ws API in backend application code (type-only import); the vulnerable path does not execute here. The finding is recorded for manifest hygiene only.\n\nNo application change required for ws itself; optionally run `npm audit fix` to bump transitive packages. If the runtime ws is required elsewhere (e.g. agentcore runtime), update that manifest to ws >= 8.21.0.",
                "markdown": "`ws` 8.19.0 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and covered by advisory CVE-2026-48779 (fixed in 8.21.0). Report filed against the sandbox-default backend manifest as instructed.\n\nNo runtime use of the ws API in backend application code (type-only import); the vulnerable path does not execute here. The finding is recorded for manifest hygiene only.\n\nNo application change required for ws itself; optionally run `npm audit fix` to bump transitive packages. If the runtime ws is required elsewhere (e.g. agentcore runtime), update that manifest to ws >= 8.21.0."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "0.0",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CVE-2026-48779",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-82662",
              "name": "CVE-2026-82662 in nodemailer 8.0.1 (SMTP transport in use)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-82662 in nodemailer 8.0.1 (SMTP transport in use)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`nodemailer` 8.0.1 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and covered by advisory CVE-2026-82662 (fixed in 8.0.8). The backend sends transactional emails with it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`nodemailer` 8.0.1 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and covered by advisory CVE-2026-82662 (fixed in 8.0.8). The backend sends transactional emails with it.\n\nThe vulnerable nodemailer code runs whenever SMTP is configured and the backend sends invite/notification emails; exploitation is confined to the email sending path.\n\nBump nodemailer to at least 8.0.8 (or the 9.x/10.x line per the other advisories) in super-agent/backend and regenerate the lockfile.",
                "markdown": "`nodemailer` 8.0.1 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and covered by advisory CVE-2026-82662 (fixed in 8.0.8). The backend sends transactional emails with it.\n\nThe vulnerable nodemailer code runs whenever SMTP is configured and the backend sends invite/notification emails; exploitation is confined to the email sending path.\n\nBump nodemailer to at least 8.0.8 (or the 9.x/10.x line per the other advisories) in super-agent/backend and regenerate the lockfile."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CVE-2026-82662",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-84504",
              "name": "CVE-2026-84504 in fastify 5.6.2 (backend HTTP framework)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "CVE-2026-84504 in fastify 5.6.2 (backend HTTP framework)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "`fastify` 5.6.2 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and covered by advisory CVE-2026-84504 (fixed in 5.12.2). Fastify is the backend's HTTP framework and processes all inbound requests."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "`fastify` 5.6.2 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and covered by advisory CVE-2026-84504 (fixed in 5.12.2). Fastify is the backend's HTTP framework and processes all inbound requests.\n\nAll inbound HTTP traffic to the backend API is handled by the vulnerable fastify version, so any request-handling flaw in the advisory is reachable by unauthenticated callers.\n\nBump fastify to at least 5.12.2 (fixes all six HIGH fastify advisories incl. CVE-2026-25223, CVE-2026-33806, CVE-2026-76169, CVE-2026-84428, CVE-2026-84469) and regenerate the lockfile.",
                "markdown": "`fastify` 5.6.2 is pinned in `super-agent/backend/package-lock.json` and covered by advisory CVE-2026-84504 (fixed in 5.12.2). Fastify is the backend's HTTP framework and processes all inbound requests.\n\nAll inbound HTTP traffic to the backend API is handled by the vulnerable fastify version, so any request-handling flaw in the advisory is reachable by unauthenticated callers.\n\nBump fastify to at least 5.12.2 (fixes all six HIGH fastify advisories incl. CVE-2026-25223, CVE-2026-33806, CVE-2026-76169, CVE-2026-84428, CVE-2026-84469) and regenerate the lockfile."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CVE-2026-84504",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
