          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "8.1",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "524864b5a8d2371cb13900edb72b3ac18fd637bdc5b4d17c4ba937cd47653f84",
            "synthetic_location": true,
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 8.1,
              "timestamp": "2026-11-20 13:17:49 UTC",
              "target": "/workspace/agentcore-superagent-update/super-agent/backend",
              "cwe": "CWE-798",
              "impact": "Complete authentication bypass on any deployment that does not set `JWT_SECRET`: the attacker authenticates as an arbitrary existing user (profile lookup by `sub`) with full `owner` role and an attacker-chosen `orgId`, granting read/write access to all organizations' data, agents, workflows, API keys, and admin functions.",
              "technical_analysis": "`src/config/index.ts:30` defines `JWT_SECRET: z.string().optional().default('super-agent-local-dev-secret-change-in-production')` — the default applies silently whenever the env var is unset, and no code path in `src/config/index.ts`, `src/app.ts`, or `src/index.ts` rejects or even warns about the default value.\n\n`src/middleware/auth.ts:62` (`verifyLocalToken`) verifies tokens with `config.jwtSecret`, and the `authenticate` hook (`src/middleware/auth.ts:157` onwards) then looks up a profile by `claims.sub` and, when no membership row exists for that `sub` (`auth.ts:230-236`), trusts the token's `role` and `orgId` claims verbatim: `role: (membership?.role as UserRole) ?? claims.role ?? 'owner'`. So a token signed with the well-known default yields a fully-authenticated `owner` user in an attacker-chosen org, for any `sub` that matches an existing profile. In local mode the only other check is profile existence.\n\n`src/routes/auth.routes.ts:47` (`POST /api/auth/login`) issues tokens signed with the same secret, confirming the value is the sole trust anchor in local mode.",
              "remediation_steps": "Remove the default value for `JWT_SECRET` in the zod schema and fail startup when it is unset (or when it equals the known default) in production; require an operator-provided secret of sufficient entropy.",
              "counterevidence": "Checked for a startup guard rejecting or warning on the default secret: none exists in `src/config/index.ts`, `src/app.ts`, or `src/index.ts` (only a CORS comment in app.ts:78). Deployment scripts `infra/scripts/deploy.sh:239` and `user-data.sh:241` do set a generated/placeholder JWT_SECRET, which limits exposure to deployments that omit it. Test setup (`tests/setup.ts`) sets its own secret. No other validation of `config.jwtSecret` exists anywhere (rg over src shows only the two use sites).",
              "confidence": "high",
              "severity_change_conditions": "Confirmation that a production deployment runs with the default secret would raise this to critical; a startup guard that refuses the default would effectively close it.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Ensure `JWT_SECRET` is unset in the backend environment (config default applies).\n2. Forge a JWT signed with the literal string `super-agent-local-dev-secret-change-in-production` containing `sub` of any existing profile, `role: owner`, and an arbitrary `orgId`.\n3. Call any authenticated API route with `Authorization: Bearer <forged>` — `authenticate` verifies it with the default secret and installs the claims as `request.user`.\n4. Executed as a vitest test importing the real `src/middleware/auth.ts` with a mocked Prisma client; all assertions passed.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        },
